      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The Osborn wave (also referred to as \"the J wave,\" \"the J deflection,\" or \"the camel's hump\") is a distinctive deflection occurring at the QRS-ST junction of approximately 80% of hypothermic patients (core body temperature < or = 95 degrees F). Generally, the amplitude and duration of Osborn waves are inversely related to core temperature. We report on eight normothermic patients whose 12-lead electrocardiograms demonstrated QRS-ST junction notches similar to those seen in hypothermia. These data support the concept that the Osborn wave is not pathognomonic of hypothermia."},
